+\section{Noise-selected cycles}\label{sec:fw}
+
+\paragraph{Resistances and potentials.} For $0<\eps<\tfrac12$ the kernel is $P_{f,\eps}(y\mid x)=\eps^{r(x,y)}(1-\eps)^{n-r(x,y)}$ with the \emph{resistance} $r(x,y)=H(F_fx,y)$, and $r(x,F_fx)=0$. Let $C_1,\dots,C_m$ be the cycles of $F_f$, $R(u\to v)$ the minimum total resistance of a path from $u$ to $v$, $V(C,C')=R(C\to C')$, and $\gamma(C)$ the minimum weight of a spanning in-tree on the cycles rooted at $C$ with edge weights $V$, the \emph{stochastic potential} \citep{Young1993}. A cycle is \emph{noise-selected} if $\gamma(C)=\min\gamma$. Write $\operatorname{ord}g$ for the exponent of the leading power of $\eps$ in $g(\eps)$.
+
+\begin{theorem}\label{thm:order}
+For every state $x$, $\operatorname{ord}\pi_{f,\eps}(x)=\min_C[\gamma(C)+R(C\to x)]-\min_C\gamma(C)$. With $D_c$ the set of states containing code $c$, $k_c=\min_C[\gamma(C)+R(C\to D_c)]-\min_C\gamma(C)$ and $k^*=\max_ck_c$: $\nu_c(f;\eps)\sim a_c\eps^{k_c}$ with $a_c>0$, and $\rho(f;\eps)\sim a\,\eps^{k^*}\log\frac1\eps$. In particular $\rho\to\infty$ iff $k^*=0$, i.e.\ iff the noise-selected cycles jointly contain all eight codes.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+By the Markov chain tree theorem \citep[Thm.~1.1]{PitmanTang2018}, $\pi_{f,\eps}(x)$ is a sum over spanning in-trees rooted at $x$ of products of transition probabilities, divided by the sum over all roots. Each product is a polynomial in $\eps$ with lowest term $\eps^{r(T)}$ and coefficient $1$, so nothing cancels and $\operatorname{ord}\pi(x)=W(x)-\min W$, $W(x)$ the minimum resistance of an in-tree rooted at $x$. For the reduction to cycles, censor the chain on $K'=\{\text{cycle states}\}\cup\{x\}$: the censored law is $\pi$ restricted to $K'$ and renormalised, $\pi(K')\to1$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:ends}, and the censored transition probabilities are convergent power series in $\eps$ with positive leading coefficient whose order is the minimum resistance of a path avoiding $K'$ in between. Apply the tree theorem on $K'$; edges inside a cycle cost $0$, so cycles contract, and shortest-path weights do not change the minimum in-tree value. In a minimum in-tree on $\{C_1,\dots,C_m,x\}$ rooted at $x$, redirect all but one of the cycles pointing to $x$ to the cycle $C_x$ that $x$ flows to at cost $0$ (keeping $C_x$ itself if it points to $x$, otherwise the cycle on $C_x$'s branch); this costs no more since $V(C',C_x)\le R(C'\to x)$ and leaves an in-tree on the cycles plus one edge into $x$. The converse inequality is concatenation of a tree and a path. Finally $\nu_c=\sum_x\pi(x)n_c(x)$ is a rational function of $\eps$ with positive leading coefficient and $\lambda(\eps)\sim\log\frac1\eps$.
+\end{proof}
+
+\paragraph{Computation.} $R(C\to\cdot)$ for all states is a 0-1 breadth-first search on the state graph (deterministic step cost $0$, single bit flip cost $1$), one per rotation orbit of cycles. All potentials $\gamma(C)$ are obtained in one pass by a root-free Edmonds contraction \citep{Edmonds1967}: it returns a laminar family of sets with dual values $y_X\ge0$ and one tight selected edge per set, and $\gamma(C)=\sum_Xy_X-\sum_{X\ni C}y_X$ for every $C$. The family is a certificate: tight selected edges forming one cycle inside every contracted set give an in-tree of that weight for every root, and dual feasibility gives the matching lower bound. All quantities are integers. For bijective $F_f$ the uniform law is stationary and $k^*=0$; for affine non-bijective $F_f$ the Walsh identity of Section~\ref{sec:results} decides uniform three-cell marginals at every $n$.
+
+\paragraph{Verification.} Before any class was classified, high-precision stationary laws (subtraction-free GTH elimination at $\eps=10^{-3},\dots,10^{-12}$) reproduced the predicted order of every state for all $256$ rules and $n\le\VerifyNmax$ (\VerifyRows{} pairs), and minimum in-trees on the full state graph reproduced the reduction to cycles exactly (\VerifyTreesRows{} cases). At $n=12$ all $256$ rules were computed independently, the $42$ classes of Lemma~\ref{lem:ends} have $k^*\ge1$ and the bijective rules $k^*=0$. An independent checker accepted all \CertsNtwelve{} certificates at $n=12$ and \CertsSweep{} certificates for $n\le14$.
+
+\paragraph{Results at $n=12$.} The $42$ classes with a code on no cycle have $k^*=1$ (\NumBkOne{} classes), $2$ (\NumBkTwo) or $3$ (\NumBkThree). Of the \NumUclasses{} classes whose cycles jointly cover all codes, \NumUrisingVtwo{} have $k^*=0$ and \NumUfallingVtwo{} have $k^*\ge1$; \NumAgreeVone{} of the $88$ classes agree with the trend classification of Section~\ref{sec:results}. The exception is class \FlipRules: \RuleOneOhSixStable{} of its \RuleOneOhSixCycles{} cycles are noise-selected and some of them contain every code, so $k^*=0$; high-precision solves give $\min_c\nu_c\to\RuleOneOhSixNuLimit$. Its maximum at $\eps^*=\RuleOneOhSixEpsStar$ is local: $\rho$ exceeds $\rho^*=\RuleOneOhSixRhoStar$ again only for $\eps$ below about $10^{\RuleOneOhSixCrossExp}$. Rules $7$, $19$, $23$ and $232$, whose trend rested on $\eps\ge0.01$, have $k^*=1$; their noise-selected cycles are the uniform states (a fixed pair or the two-cycle between them).
+
+\paragraph{Ring size.} For $n=\SweepNmin,\dots,\SweepNmax$, \SweepAllZero{} classes have $k^*=0$ and \SweepAllFall{} have $k^*\ge1$ at every $n\ge8$; the other \SweepVaryCount{} (classes \SweepVaryRules) change side with $n$, several of them according to the divisibility of $n$. For the affine classes $60$ and $90$ the change is proved: $k^*\ge1$ exactly when $n$ is a power of two, where the global map is nilpotent.
+
 \section{What is established, and what is not}
 
-Established exactly for $n=12$: the zero-noise classes and closure times for all $256\times4096$ pairs; the rate curves $\rho(f;\eps)$ on the grid for all classes, with the resolved/unresolved status of every entry; $\eps^*$ and $\rho^*$ for every class with an interior maximum; the finite-horizon margins for all initial states at the listed $\eps$ and $T$; the boundary check. Proved for all $n$: Lemmas~\ref{lem:decomp}--\ref{lem:ends}. Observed, not proved: unimodality of the curves; the classification of the \NumClassesUndet{} classes whose cycles jointly cover all codes, which rests on the trend of $\rho$ between the two smallest resolved noise levels (at most \TrendSecondEpsMax; above $0.001$ only for rules \TrendLateRules) and would need the large-deviation weights of the cycles for a proof. Not claimed: anything about $n\ne12$ (the attractor structure of elementary automata changes with the ring size \citep{WuenscheLesser1992}), about observers who do not know $\eps$ or see only part of the ring, or about generators outside the family beyond the three checks of Section~\ref{sec:boundary}.
+Established exactly for $n=12$: the zero-noise classes and closure times for all $256\times4096$ pairs; the rate curves $\rho(f;\eps)$ on the grid for all classes, with the resolved/unresolved status of every entry; $\eps^*$ and $\rho^*$ for every class with an interior maximum; the finite-horizon margins for all initial states at the listed $\eps$ and $T$; the boundary check. New in version 0.2, established exactly with checked certificates: the noise-selected cycles, $k_c$ and $k^*$ for all $88$ classes at $n=12$ (which settles the classification left open by version 0.1, including rules $7$, $19$, $23$, $232$, and moves class \FlipRules{} to the rising side) and for $n=\SweepNmin,\dots,\SweepNmax$. Proved for all $n$: Lemmas~\ref{lem:decomp}--\ref{lem:ends} and Theorem~\ref{thm:order}; $k^*=0$ for bijective rules; the power-of-two criterion for classes $60$ and $90$. Observed, not proved: unimodality of the curves; every other dependence of the classification on $n$, which is computed for $n\le\SweepNmax$ only. Not claimed: the classification for $n>\SweepNmax$ outside the affine and bijective classes; the leading coefficient $a$ of $\rho$, which decides where the asymptotic regime begins (for class \FlipRules{} far below any practical noise level); observers who do not know $\eps$ or see only part of the ring; generators outside the family beyond the three checks of Section~\ref{sec:boundary}.
 
 The practical reading is narrow: for a deterministic system whose attractor exercises only part of its rule, a known amount of dynamical noise is an instrument, not a nuisance, and the right amount can be computed from the rule's stationary neighbourhood frequencies. For a system whose attractor already exercises the whole rule, noise is only a nuisance. Which of the two one is facing can be read off the stationary neighbourhood frequencies; whether the family is the right one cannot be read off the posterior over it.
 
